Do not take the Linux-on-Z path on z/OS.

numpy treats __s390x__ as implying Linux on Z: it includes <sys/auxv.h> and
reads CPU features from the auxiliary vector via getauxval(AT_HWCAP). z/OS is
s390x but has no auxiliary vector and ships no sys/auxv.h, so the branch cannot
compile there -- "fatal error: 'sys/auxv.h' file not found".

Excluding z/OS lets it fall through to the unsupported-arch path that numpy
already maintains, which reports no CPU features rather than failing to build.

diff --git a/numpy/_core/src/common/npy_cpu_features.c b/numpy/_core/src/common/npy_cpu_features.c
--- a/numpy/_core/src/common/npy_cpu_features.c
+++ b/numpy/_core/src/common/npy_cpu_features.c
@@ -714,7 +714,10 @@
 
 /***************** ZARCH ******************/
 
-#elif defined(__s390x__)
+#elif defined(__s390x__) && !defined(__MVS__)
+/* z/OS is s390x but has no auxiliary vector: no sys/auxv.h and no getauxval(),
+   so it falls through to the unsupported-arch path below, which reports no CPU
+   features rather than failing to compile. */
 
 #include <sys/auxv.h>
 
